ory, and are standardized to the age distribution of the study population over follow up from 1980 to 1990. The lowest, middle, and highest categories are three of the five consumption categories used in Tables 2 to 4. <sup>a</sup>At age 20 to 35 years.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="T2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Rate ratios of rheumatoid arthritis by protein and iron intakes among women in the Nurses' Health Study, 1980 to 2002</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="left">Nutrients</td><td align="left">Quintile</td><td align="left">Median intake</td><td align="left">Number of cases</td><td align="left">Age-adjusted<sup>a </sup>rate ratio (95% CI)</td><td align="left">Multivariate<sup>b </sup>rate ratio (95% CI)</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Total protein<sup>c</sup></td><td align="left">1</td><td align="left">60.8 g/day</td><td align="left">95</td><td align="left">1.00</td><td align="left">1.00</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">2</td><td align="left">68.4 g/day</td><td align="left">109</td><td align="left">1.12 (0.86&#x02013;1.48)</td><td align="left">1.11 (0.84, 1.47)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">3</td><td align="left">73.9 g/day</td><td align="left">89</td><td align="left">0.90 (0.67&#x02013;1.20)</td><td align="left">0.90 (0.67, 1.21)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">4</td><td align="left">79.5 g/day</td><td align="left">133</td><td align="left">1.35 (1.04&#x02013;1.76)</td><td align="left">1.34 (1.03, 1.76)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">5</td><td align="left">89.0 g/day</td><td align="left">120</td><td align="left">1.23 (0.94&#x02013;1,61)</td><td align="left">1.17 (0.88, 1.55)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td align="left"><italic>P </italic>for trend = 0.04<sup>d</sup></td><td align="left"><italic>P </italic>for trend = 0.12<sup>d</sup></td></tr><tr><td align="left">Animal protein<sup>e</sup></td><td align="left">1</td><